Z||000000||Coop88||05-11-2001||05:21 PM||||When I burn out the rubber bushings, can I just let the fire burn them out completely? Or do I just need to get the inner sleeve out and cut the rest away? I'm installing poly.<p>[This message has been edited by Coop88 (edited 05-11-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Bob Englert||05-11-2001||10:28 PM||||I would let the torch do as much work as possible.  When they are out clean up the outer shell with a wire brush and then some fine sandpaper.   <P>Lube the heck out of the sleeve when installing the poly bushings.<P><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||Coop88||05-12-2001||02:29 PM||||I've got the rubber buhings out. Now I'm cleaning the sleeves. The guy across the street told me to soak the ends of the control arm in gasoline. Anyone ever try this? Also, how do you get the shafts out of the upper control arms? The bushings came out fine, but I can't figure out how to get the shafts out (this is an 88 formula). What's the secret?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||05-12-2001||10:50 PM||||When I did the poly bushings on my Formula (now sold) I left the shaft in the control arm.  The poly bushings install from the outside anyway, so there is no need to remove the shaft from the control arm.<P>I don't think I I ever figured out out to get it out either!<P><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000004||Coop88||05-13-2001||03:37 AM||||I'm gonna grind the shaft until it's thin enough to slip through the bushing sleeves. I just hope the shafts from the Fiero Store will fit. I've got my fingers crossed.||||reg||1
